ademy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d072232f3ad8a49ff32334e626619bf744c2b93b2dacf8a0d6a3562c3a3f5c1" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83697" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d072232f3ad8a49ff32334e626619bf744c2b93b2dacf8a0d6a3562c3a3f5c1" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83697" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d072232f3ad8a49ff32334e626619bf744c2b93b2dacf8a0d6a3562c3a3f5c1" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d072232f3ad8a49ff32334e626619bf744c2b93b2dacf8a0d6a3562c3a3f5c1" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d072232f3ad8a49ff32334e626619bf744c2b93b2dacf8a0d6a3562c3a3f5c1" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/activity-metadata.j