ake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d072232f3ad8a49ff32334e626619bf744c2b93b2dacf8a0d6a3562c3a3f5c1" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d072232f3ad8a49ff32334e626619bf744c2b93b2dacf8a0d6a3562c3a3f5c1" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d072232f3ad8a49ff32334e626619bf744c2b93b2dacf8a0d6a3562c3a3f5c1" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fba20b775935b352073a60c9f679b7174472b2490f4ea3737700991b80c2658" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83854" "f