trix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d072232f3ad8a49ff32334e626619bf744c2b93b2dacf8a0d6a3562c3a3f5c1" "basis" 2418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linea